{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Each man according to the interpretation of his dream.</b> The expression ״פִּתְרוֹן חֲלוֹמוֹת״ (the interpretation of dreams) refers to the event that will happen to him in the future, and the one who makes known that future is called the פּוֹתֵר (interpreter). According to the view of many (Ibn Ezra and Radak), פִּתְרוֹן means explanation, and the sense of ״אִישׁ כְּפִתְרוֹן חֲלוֹמוֹ״ is: each and every one dreamed a dream that resembled the interpretation that would come to him. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 40:5). But what reason would the chief butler have to say to Pharaoh (Genesis 41:11), ״We dreamed a dream resembling the interpretation״? — for in saying this he would diminish the wisdom of the interpreter, and perhaps Pharaoh's dream would not be so, and Joseph would not know it. Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 40:5) said that each one saw in his dream what the interpretation of his dream would be — everything that would happen to him in the future — meaning that it was a true dream, not coming with an excess of matter such that only part of it would be fulfilled. And this is the correct view."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {0 "The phrase 'חָלַם חֲלוֹם הַדּוֹמֶה לַפִּתְרוֹן' in the middle of the comment paraphrases the chief butler's words in Genesis 41:11, applying Rashi's interpretation to that verse as an objection; I have rendered it as a rhetorical question to preserve the argumentative structure."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:16.618862259Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}